WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
In this problem, a building is undergoing construction.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.439
A 20-foot beam used to hold 40 kips in its center.

00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:16.559
The point load is being removed and the engineer wants to determine the equivalent distributed

00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:21.679
force per linear foot that can be applied to the beam instead.

00:00:21.679 --> 00:00:30.480
The equivalent force in kips per foot is most nearly 1, 2, 3 or 4.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.960
So in this problem, we have a beam that used to receive a point force that we want to convert

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.399
into an equivalent distributed load.

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:46.640
So what we can do is first go to chapter 4 of our civil reference handbook, which will

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:58.280
be pages 236 or 238, or if you're going by the PDF page numbers, it's 248 and 250.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.359
So the list of beams provided there gives you the sheer diagrams for each of those types

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:07.560
of beams as well as the equations.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:15.640
So if we were to go to number 7 first, 7 is our simple beam with a point load at center.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:26.879
Alright, so that case looks like the first one over here and we can see that the equation

00:01:26.879 --> 00:01:34.640
provided for sheer in this case, it's using r is equal to v, which is sheer, which is equal

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.879
to the point load over 2.

00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:54.519
So in this case, sheer is going to be equal to the load of 40 kips over 2, so 20 kips.

00:01:54.519 --> 00:02:00.280
Now if we were to look at the other diagram of what the distributed load looks like for

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:07.599
the sheer, that's going to be the first one listed on page 236 or 248, depending

00:02:07.599 --> 00:02:12.560
on if you're going by the book pages or the PDF pages.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:23.079
And that is given as a simple beam with a uniformly distributed load.

00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:29.719
So the max sheer in this case is going to be w, which is the distributed load, l, which

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:35.039
is the length of the beam over 2 minus x.

00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:40.719
So if we look at the sheer diagram for this beam, we can see that the max sheer is going

00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:43.719
to occur at the edges.

00:02:43.719 --> 00:03:00.239
So in this case, x is going to be equal to 0, max sheer.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.480
So in this case, it's just w, l over 2.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.800
And we've already been given the max sheer that we need to account for from the previous

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.960
beam type.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:18.320
So what we're going to do now is just plug in our equation and finish the problem.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.439
So 20 kips equals w, which is what we're solving for.

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:29.520
l is 20 feet and just over 2.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.600
So in this case, w equals 2 kips per foot.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.360
And that's all it takes to solve this problem.
